				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\Delta {G}_{{bind}}=\Delta H-T\Delta S\approx \Delta {E}_{{MM}}+\Delta {G}_{{sol}}-T\Delta S,$$\end{document}ΔGbind=ΔH−TΔS≈ΔEMM+ΔGsol−TΔS,3\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\Delta {E}_{{MM}}=\Delta {E}_{{internal}}+\Delta {E}_{{electrostatic}}+\Delta {E}_{{vdw}};\Delta {G}_{{sol}}=\Delta {G}_{{PB}/{GB}}+\Delta {G}_{{SA}},$$\end{document}ΔEMM=ΔEinternal+ΔEelectrostatic+ΔEvdw;ΔGsol=ΔGPB/GB+ΔGSA,where ∆EMM, ∆Gsol, and -T∆S are the changes in the molecular mechanics energy, solvation-free energy, and conformational entropy upon binding, respectively. ∆EMM includes ∆Einternal (bond, angle, and dihedral energies), electrostatic, and van der Waals energies. ∆Gsol is the sum of the electrostatic solvation energy, ∆GPB/GB (polar contribution), and non-electrostatic solvation, ∆GSA (non-polar contribution). The polar contribution was calculated by using the generalized Born model, while the non-polar energy was calculated by the solvent accessible surface area (SASA). The VSGB 2.0164 solvation model and OPLS force field were used for these calculations. Residues located within 5 Å from the ligands were included in the flexible region, and the rest of the protein atoms were kept frozen.
